{
  "text": "When thin flat or curved structural elements buckle, an extra stress pattern is added to the original one. If this extra pattern is assumed to depend only on the amount of stored shear energy at each point, the analysis shows that treating the material as undergoing plastic deformation predicts lower critical buckling loads than treating it as flowing plastically. Applying the plastic‑deformation view yields predictions that match well with several experimental data sets. The greatest increase of the critical load over the purely elastic value occurs when the shape of the stress deviator caused by buckling is most different from the original stress deviator. Stress‑strain equations are obtained by expressing infinitesimal extra strains as total differentials and differentiating strains with respect to stresses. A differential equation governing buckling of flat elements is derived and solved for various loading and support conditions, with results presented in tables. A similar formulation is provided for curved elements, and the outcomes are compared with other existing theoretical approaches."
